A||||12||Stan86SE||Ignition Module Questions||||5||
Z||000000||Stan86SE||03-01-2001||07:31 PM||||I went to install a new ignition module this afternoon.  The two wires coming from the pickup coil have no plastic connector "shell".  The pickup wires DO have the female metal connectors on the ends, but that's it.  Will not having the plastic connector shell cause me any problems?<P>Also, the ignition module came with the little packet of silicone.  Is using just the silicone enough, or do I also need to use dielectric compound?  (The ignition module instructions only mention using the silicone; my manuals talk about also using the dielectric compound. ???)<P>Thanks for your help,<BR>Stan <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||03-01-2001||07:39 PM||||That IS the dielectric...<P>can't help with the wires.... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Stan86SE||03-01-2001||07:48 PM||||Ha, ha!  Thanks, Ogre!  That's what I thought, but being (obviously) a shade tree kinda guy, I though I'd better play it safe and check.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Stan86SE (edited 03-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Stan86SE||03-01-2001||08:29 PM||||Does anybody think that the pickup coil wire ends could just be covered with something like electrical heat shrink tubing, or is insulating them even necessary?  I don't want to replace the pickup coil unless I absolutely have to...<P>Thanks,<BR>Stan||||reg||5
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||03-01-2001||09:03 PM||||It <B>is</B> better to isolate the wires and electrical heat shrink tubing might do it. Perhaps it's even better to use some high temperature silicone to seal it, but that's a bit messy.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Stan86SE||03-01-2001||09:13 PM||||Yeah, Cliff, high-temp silicone probably is a better idea, considering the heat. Thanks for the input.<P>-Stan||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||03-02-2001||06:01 AM||||id get a new pick up coil it will have the connector on it. You can replace it (have to take out distributor) or just cut off and splice the wires with the new connector. I think it was only like $12||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||03-02-2001||02:49 PM||||I <B>think</B> the pickup coil doesn't care about polarity (not absolutely sure) - in a pinch, I'd be willing to hook it up to the ignition module without the plastic connector (cover).  The car will probably run, so you can drive it to a parts store, or even a day or two until you can fix it right.<P>But in the end, if the pickup coil has seen enough heat to crumble the plastic connector away, it's probably a good idea to replace the pickup coil anyway.  I tend to skimp on my own cars, but even I would do the replacement.  Relatively cheap insurance. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Stan86SE||03-02-2001||07:49 PM||||Thanks, Roger and StuGood.  I have no problem at all with the minor *cost* of replacing the pickup coil.  I just didn't wanna have to pull the distributor.  Funny thing is, the Chilton manual (I also have the Haynes) says that you DON'T have to remove the distributor on 85-88 HEI's to replace the pickup coil.  But, I'll be #$@%'ed if their instructions for replacing the PU coil match what I'm seeing...<P>I hooked up the new ignition module with the polarity that the old one had.  There was no sign at all of their ever having been a plastic connector cover on this PU coil's wires, and I've been driving the car 5 years with no problems until just lately.  In the last month, I've replaced the O2, fuel pump relay, the EGR, EGR solenoid, oil sending unit, and now the ignition module.  Plugs, wires, cap, and rotor are all just a few months old.  The engine is still bogging down and backfiring on acceleration.  If I 'ease' into giving it fuel, it'll usually do alright.  But, I still don't trust it to not stall in traffic or leave me stranded.  I haven't checked, because I don't have a fuel pressure gauge (YET), but now I'm just about back to what I thought the problem was in the first place...possibly a clogged fuel filter/line or a failing fuel pump.  Along with changing out that pickup coil...<P>My apologies for the LONNNG post!!!    <p>[This message has been edited by Stan86SE (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cliff Pennock||03-02-2001||08:16 PM||||Does it still die on you after you've replaced the ignition module?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Stan86SE||03-02-2001||09:20 PM||||Cliff, I apologize for the length of this post, too, but I guess I can do 10 short posts, or one _really_ long one!  You'll see the word "sometimes" used a lot here, but that's the way things are happening.<P>Even with the new ignition module, here's what's happening:  It'll start up okay, sometimes die and have to be restarted or sometimes idle fine at around 950-1050.  Then, while idling, sometimes it will suddenly swoop down to 500 and stall or try to stall and recover.  I can give it some gas a little at a time when it does that, and sometimes it'll idle okay again or sometimes it'll start acting like it's flooded and finally stall again.  If it stalls, sometimes it'll crank, but not start for awhile.  <P>Even when it is running, like I said earlier, it wants to bog down and/or backfire upon acceleration.  Or, even bog down if I rev it at idle.  Here's the history of how this all started a few months ago and how things have progressed:<P>*First symptom I had:  Start, then dies.  Start again, everything back to normal.<P>*Second "stage":  Start, hit the road, stumbling and backfiring in the first mile or so, acting like it's flooded or fuel-starved.  It finally recovers, then all is okay.  Not long after this stuff starts, it starts throwing intermittent 44's while at interstate cruising speed (70 mph).<P>*Third stage:  Starts idling erratically, steady idle at first, then hunting from 500-1500, throws intermittent 44's, coughs and dies.<P>*Fourth stage:  I do all the usual 44 troubleshooting, end up replacing the EGR valve, the 44's disappear. Idle not completely normal, but *much* better.  <P>*Fifth stage:  Starts throwing 32, replace the EGR solenoid, 32 disappears. On long drives, everything seems normal, except for the occasional backfire on takeoff and occasional bogging down when I get on it a bit or try to kick in the passing gear.<P>*Sixth stage: Basically running okay except for the occasional backfiring and stumbling/hesitation on acceleration, but now it stalls sometimes and I have to sit awhile before it'll start again.  Get stranded on three different occasions:  twice after having driven awhile and then shutting it off (it would then crank, but not start - would start again after waiting 15-30 minutes); and a stall once while driving (same story...wouldn't start again for 30-40 minutes.)<P>This is now.  I replaced the ignition module and also the oil sending unit today.  Having to start it twice sometimes before it'll hold an idle.  Still bogging down bad on acceleration, still dying at idle sometimes (will idle just fine, then swoop down to 400-500, sputter and die), then sometimes hard to start again, acting like it's either flooded or fuel-starved (take your pick   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ). I don't trust the car enough yet to take it out in traffic again.<P>Again - my apologies for this "novel"!<P>Thanks,<BR>Stan<BR>    <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Stan86SE (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cliff Pennock||03-02-2001||10:15 PM||||What happens when you disconnect the tachfilter? Does that make any difference?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Stan86SE||03-02-2001||11:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B>What happens when you disconnect the tachfilter? Does that make any difference?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'll have to check that out tomorrow, when I have some light, Cliff.<P>I must have a heckuva vacuum leak somewhere.  I just went out and started the car and let it run for about 10 minutes.  It started right up, then died.  Took 3 more tries to get it started and riding the gas pedal to keep it running.  When it finally did idle, it went into a high idle and stayed there - 16-18.  I kicked it down, put it in gear, and drove it around nearby...no more than a quarter mile.  Pulled back into my parking spot, put it in park, and it went right back to the high idle.  I popped the deck lid to look at the engine because of the sound of it, and my rear exhaust manifold was glowing bright red.  I immediately shut it down.  Looks like I've got some hunting to do.  I've got the steel vacuum lines coming from Rodney Dickman supposedly next week.  That can't hurt for a start.  Man, this #@$% is getting *really* old...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ||||reg||1
